## Interface the worker method and the skill scripts call (crate mixdog-office, `mixdog_office::engine`)
- `Platform` (trait; `Send + Sync + 'static`) is what a process supplies: `lanes()`, `office() -> &OfficeCom`, `fonts() -> Arc<mixdog_fonts::registry::Registry>`,
  `font_env() -> &FontEnv`, `svg_fonts() -> &SvgFonts`, `endpoint() -> &dyn BrowserEndpoint`, `design_environment() -> &mixdog_office_design::library::Environment`,
  `fetch() -> &dyn Fetch`, `indexer() -> &dyn TemplateIndexer<Unlaned>`, `validator() -> &mixdog_ooxml::ooxml_validator::Environment`, and
  `batch_host() -> &Self::Batch` (`type Batch: BatchHost + Send + Sync`, the PDF operation set, the Word chart as a picture and the brief readings).
  The real ones exist in the tree (`HttpsFetch`, `OfficeTemplateIndexer`, `Environment::process()`, `Environment::from_process()`); the process that owns the
  Office transport and the shared browser (the ext-host worker, nr-office-worker-office-run) builds the platform. No platform is composed here.
- `Engine::new(platform) -> Arc<Engine<P>>`; `Engine::execute(self: &Arc<Self>, args: Value, cwd: PathBuf, data_dir: Option<PathBuf>, ledger:
  Option<Arc<CallLedger>>, cancel: CancellationToken) -> (Value, Vec<FileChange>)` is `executeOfficeTool`: the tool result (`{ content: [...], isError? }`) and
  the workspace files the call's write routes changed (`CallLedger::changes`, for the turn review; pass `None` outside a session's turn). An empty `cwd` is the
  process's folder, no `data_dir` is the Office data folder. The approved-path check stays in the worker method, as in methods.mjs. A cancelled call answers
  `{ ok: false, code: 'cancelled' }`; an `OfficeConflictError` its details; any other failure `Error: <message>`. `MIXDOG_OFFICE_TRACE` writes one line per call.
  The call runs on a thread of its own at normal priority (the engine's contexts are generic over the file-I/O proof, and the engine gives the call `Unlaned`, as the
  worker's other document methods do) and is driven there on a parker of its own; the render and author steps that belong to a lane are lane jobs the call submits and
  finishes one at a time, so no lane thread is held across another lane job. Call it from inside the tokio runtime that owns the Office transport.
- `Engine::known_sessions()` before a call and `Engine::finish_call(thread, &known) -> bool` after it are the pin rule (the worker method reports pin while a
  thread owns a session); `Engine::is_pinned(thread)`; `Engine::shutdown()` drains every Microsoft Office session host (`closeOfficeSessions`).
- `Engine::execute` keeps what a session was last sent of its render receipt across calls (`deliveries`) by moving it into the call and back; two calls that
  run at the same time on different threads may therefore each start from the receipts the other left (a receipt is then sent in full once more).
- `mixdog_office::actions::Machine` is what one call runs with (a test builds its own; `twin_dispatch` does): `run_office_tool(&args, signal) -> Value` is
  `runOfficeTool`; `dispatch_session_action`, `qa`, `finalize`, `save`, `close` and `open_create_or_attach` are its actions. `Host` is `OfficeHost + ComHost +
  ComSessions` (`ComSessions::close_session` is `closeMicrosoftOfficeSession`; `OfficeCom` implements it); `engine::Closer` is the `SessionCloser` the author and
  create paths use; `engine::AuthoringReads` is the readings a deck's design review is held to.
- The crate's `README`-level behaviours that stay the caller's: a thread's pin and the approved-path check (worker), the turn-review recording of the changes
  (`recordToolFileChanges`, tools layer).

## What was not compiled or run
Nothing was compiled or run in Rust. The places most likely to need a touch at the first compile: the lifetimes of `Machine` and of the per-call contexts in
`Engine::run_call` (one `'a` for every borrowed context, as `SystemAuthor` has), the generic bounds repeated on the `impl` blocks of `Machine`, the
`BrowserEndpoint` stand-in of `tests/twin_dispatch.rs` (written without the `async_trait` macro, in the shape the macro generates) and `Platform::Batch`.
`twin_live_dispatch` (ignored) re-records through Node and was not run in Rust; the recorder itself was run once (Node only) and produced the committed recording.

## The skill scripts (nr-exthost-skill-scripts-office, section 3 of its wire)
Its `OfficeEngine::call(args, cwd)` is `Engine::execute(args, cwd, None, None, CancellationToken::new())` with the answer's `content[0].text` parsed (or, when
`isError`, returned as the error text); `measure` stays the portable readers' own. No platform is composed in this task, so that adapter is written where the
process that runs `mixdogd skill-run` builds its `Platform`.
